<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Balthasar Neumann</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1687–1753</span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Balthasar Neumann was a German architect, the leading master of the late baroque in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. Born in Cheb, he worked as an engineer and architect for the Schönborn prince-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wurzburg">Würzburg</a>, for whom he designed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wurzburg-residence">Würzburg Residence</a>. His other works include the pilgrimage church of Vierzehnheiligen, the abbey church of Neresheim and grand staircases at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bruchsal">Bruchsal</a> and Brühl.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/balthasar-neumann.jpg" alt="Balthasar Neumann" width="320" height="369" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Marcus Friedrich Kleinert (1694-1742) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Balthasar_Neumann_W%C3%BCrzburg_Mainfr%C3%A4nkisches_Museum_Marienberg.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
